  "history": "Knife came into late Old English, probably from Old Norse knífr, another word from the time of the Vikings. The older English word for a knife was seax, and some people say the Saxons got their name from this kind of knife, but that is not certain. In the past, the k in knife was spoken: ‘k-nife’. In the 1600s the k at the start of words like knife, knee and know went silent, but the spelling stayed. The word also travelled south: French canif, a small folding knife, was borrowed from Middle English. The plural is knives: like wife and wives, life and lives, the f changes to v.",
  "historyEinfach": "Knife is a Viking word. Long ago, people said the k: ‘k-nife’. Later the k became silent, but we still write it. The same happened in know and knee. One knife, two knives: f becomes v.",
